add(1, '''
Sapir's perspective on language | A characterization emphasizing human symbolic communication of ideas, emotions and desires. | A definition groups communicating ideas with expressing feelings and wishes through symbols. | Sapir's definition in the handout defines language solely as a set of mathematical sentences. | This is a paraphrase of the handout's historical definition, not an endorsement that natural signed languages should be excluded.
Bloch and Trager's perspective | A characterization emphasizing arbitrary vocal symbols enabling social cooperation. | A definition relates a group's cooperation to a conventional system of speech symbols. | Bloch and Trager's definition in the handout excludes any role for a social group. | Its vocal emphasis reflects a historical formulation; contemporary accounts include signed languages.
Hall's perspective on language | A characterization emphasizing language as an institution for human communication and interaction using habitual symbols. | A definition highlights an institution through which humans interact by established oral-auditory symbols. | Hall's historical definition in the handout concerns only spelling and printing. | The historical oral-auditory emphasis should be distinguished from the broader modern recognition of signed language.
Chomsky's formal characterization | A characterization of a language as a set of sentences constructed from a finite inventory of elements. | A finite vocabulary and structural resources support potentially unbounded sentence formation. | A potentially infinite language requires each individual sentence to contain infinitely many words. | An unbounded set can consist of individual sentences that are each finite in length.
Iconicity | A motivated resemblance between aspects of a linguistic form and its meaning. | An onomatopoeic word imitates an associated sound to some degree. | The presence of iconic forms proves that no other linguistic signs can be arbitrary. | Iconicity and convention can coexist; sound-symbolic forms differ across languages.
''')
add(2, '''
Macrolinguistics | A broad teaching label for investigation of language in relation to wider social, psychological or other domains. | A course groups sociolinguistics and psycholinguistics as analyses beyond internal structure alone. | Macrolinguistics means that core language structures are irrelevant to all interdisciplinary work. | Micro/macro terminology is useful for orientation but its boundaries vary across textbooks.
Language planning | Deliberate efforts to influence a community's language practices, status or resources. | A policy develops terminology and literacy materials for a regional language. | Language planning concerns only an individual's tongue position during one consonant. | Status planning and corpus planning address different but connected dimensions of language policy.
Register | Variation in language associated with a communicative situation, purpose or activity. | The same speaker uses different wording in a research article and a family message. | Register is determined solely by a speaker's birthplace and cannot change with context. | Register differs from regional dialect, although both can shape a text.
''')
add(3, '''
Postalveolar place | Articulation just behind the alveolar ridge. | English [ʃ] in ship is commonly described as postalveolar. | English [ʃ] must be produced with both lips fully closed. | Some introductory books use broader palatal labels; more precise IPA descriptions separate postalveolar and palatal places.
Palatal place | Articulation involving the tongue body and the hard palate. | [j] in yes is a palatal approximant. | A palatal consonant is defined by contact between the lower lip and upper teeth. | Palatal [j] differs in place from the velar consonant [k].
Lateral approximant | An approximant whose airflow passes around one or both sides of the tongue. | English [l] allows lateral oral airflow. | A lateral approximant requires all airflow to pass only through the nose. | Lateral refers to airflow; English /l/ can have different context-dependent realizations.
Aspiration | A period of voiceless airflow associated with the release of a stop, marked [ʰ] in IPA. | The [pʰ] in a typical stressed English pin has an audible puff of air. | Aspiration is identical to the voiced vibration of [z]. | In English it is often predictable for voiceless stops; its contrastive status differs across languages.
Syllable structure | Organization of segments into units commonly analyzed as onset, nucleus and coda. | Cat has /k/ as onset, /æ/ as nucleus and /t/ as coda in a simple analysis. | Every syllable in every language must contain an overt consonant onset. | The nucleus is the sonority peak; some languages permit syllabic consonants.
Amplitude and intensity | Related physical measures associated with signal magnitude and energy. | A sound recording displays greater waveform excursions when amplitude increases. | Greater amplitude by definition means that a word has more morphemes. | Loudness is a perceptual correlate, not simply another name for a physical amplitude measurement.
Duration | The time interval occupied by an event or speech segment. | A vowel lasts 150 milliseconds in a recorded token. | Duration is measured in hertz rather than units of time. | Physical duration can affect contrasts, stress and rhythm, depending on the language.
''')
add(4, '''
Grammatical rank scale | A hierarchy of grammatical units in a stated framework. | Sentence, clause, phrase, word and morpheme are arranged from larger to smaller units in an introductory scale. | Syntax itself is a grammatical unit on the same rank scale as word. | Syntax names a field or level of analysis, not a unit equivalent to sentence on this scale.
Open lexical class | A word class that readily admits new members. | English gains new nouns and verbs for new technologies. | An open lexical class is defined by being unable to gain new words. | Openness is a tendency; nouns, verbs and adjectives are common examples.
Closed lexical class | A word class with relatively limited membership and limited ready addition. | English personal pronouns form a comparatively restricted inventory. | A closed class can never change in any way over historical time. | Closed means comparatively resistant to expansion, not eternally frozen.
English inflection inventory | The conventional introductory list of eight English inflectional suffix categories. | Plural, possessive, third-person singular, past tense, two participles, comparative and superlative are listed. | Agentive -er in teacher belongs to the eight ordinary English inflectional suffix categories. | Comparative -er in taller is inflectional; the similar-looking agentive -er is derivational.
Blending | Forming a word by combining portions of existing words. | Brunch combines parts of breakfast and lunch. | Blending requires retaining both complete words in every case. | It differs from ordinary compounding in which whole lexical bases are combined.
Clipping | Forming a shorter lexical form by shortening an existing word. | Exam is a shortened form of examination. | Clipping requires adding a new derivational suffix. | Shortening does not necessarily change the word's core referential meaning or category.
Acronym formation | Forming a word from initial letters that is pronounced as a word in a narrow usage. | NASA is pronounced as a word rather than as a sequence of individual letter names. | Every initial-letter abbreviation must be pronounced as one word. | Terminology varies; the narrow contrast distinguishes acronyms from initialisms such as BBC.
Borrowing | Adoption of a linguistic form from another language. | English uses bungalow, historically borrowed from an Indian-language source. | A borrowed word must permanently retain every phonetic property of its source language. | Borrowings often become adapted to the receiving language's sounds and morphology.
''')
add(5, '''
Linguistic theory | An explicit framework aiming to describe or explain linguistic patterns. | A grammar proposes structural representations and tests whether they account for speaker judgments. | A linguistic theory is simply any unsupported personal preference about elegant writing. | Theoretical assumptions should be distinguished from observations and evaluated against evidence.
Accent | Patterns of pronunciation associated with an individual or variety. | Two speakers use similar grammar but pronounce the same vowels differently. | Accent necessarily includes every difference in vocabulary and clause structure. | Dialect is a broader label; everyone has an accent in the descriptive sense.
Sociology of language | Investigation emphasizing how language relates to the organization and functioning of society. | A study examines institutional language distribution in education and administration. | Sociology of language is defined only by measuring the acoustic spectrum of vowels. | It overlaps sociolinguistics, but often foregrounds societal institutions and language functions.
''')
add(7, '''
Sense | An expression's linguistic meaning and its relations within a language, distinguished from particular reference. | Morning star and evening star differ in sense even when both refer to Venus. | Two expressions with the same referent must have identical sense. | Co-reference does not require identical descriptive or cognitive content.
Reference | The use of an expression to identify an entity in context. | A speaker uses that student to identify a particular person in the room. | Reference is identical to every emotional association a hearer has with a word. | Referential interpretation draws on linguistic meaning and contextual information.
Analytic statement | A statement true in virtue of meanings under an adopted semantic analysis. | Every bachelor is unmarried is an introductory example using the ordinary adult-person sense. | An analytic statement is defined as one requiring a survey to discover whether its words mean what they do. | Examples depend on the stipulated sense; social and institutional uses can complicate apparently simple definitions.
''')
